  {
   "text": "Equipment is an artifact subtype that has the \" equip \" keyword ability . By paying the equip cost (which can only be done at sorcery speed ), it attaches to a creature you control. If that creature leaves play, the Equipment becomes unattached and remains in play. Paying the equip cost also allows the Equipment to be moved from one creature to another.",
   "scene": 0,
   "section": ""
  },
  {
   "text": "Equipment was created to improve the concept of creature enchantments—specifically, the inherent card disadvantage of attaching auras to a creature. First appearing throughout Mirrodin block , that block's heavy artifact theme presented an opportunity to represent a creature's magical weapons and devices as artifacts instead of enchantments, and introduce new rules for using them. Equipment was introduced to core sets in 9th Edition .",
   "scene": 1,
   "section": "History"
  },
  {
   "text": "Alara Reborn introduced the first colored Equipment; 12 years later, Zendikar Rising introduced equipment with colored equip costs. Zendikar Rising also expanded on \"snap-on equipment\", which attaches itself to a target creature upon entering , first seen on Piston Sledge . These acted closer to Auras, as they had a stronger up-front impact, in exchange for higher equip costs for their longevity of impact.",
   "scene": 1,
   "section": "History"
  },
  {
   "text": "Scars of Mirrodin introduced the mechanic Living weapon , which had Equipment that played like value creatures rather than as support spells. It involved creating a 0/0 token that attached the equipment to it. It would be later repeated with For Mirrodin! and Job select , using differently sized tokens.",
   "scene": 1,
   "section": "History"
  },
  {
   "text": "Kamigawa: Neon Dynasty introduced equipment that are also artifact creatures, alongside a new keyword for them in Reconfigure , allowing them to change forms.",
   "scene": 1,
   "section": "History"
  },
  {
   "text": "Equipment can take many different forms of usable items ranging from weapons, armor, clothing, books, etc. They are seen both on cards of the type itself as well as in the art of cards of other types. Common across most planes are bladed weapons like swords or daggers, blunt weapons like maces, as well as ranged weapons like bows & arrows.",
   "scene": 3,
   "section": "Aesthetic design"
  },
  {
   "text": "The Edge featured some unique technological weapons such as Bladiators, hardlight blades edged with blue oxygen plasma capable of close-quarters fighting in short-axe mode or emitting blasts of solar energy at range, and gravblades - weapons that use gravity as a weapon.",
   "scene": 3,
   "section": "Aesthetic design"
  },
  {
   "text": "Defensive equipment includes armor ( 5,762 cards ) and shields ( 1,354 cards ). The Abzan are known to wear a special style of ancestral armor. Universes Beyond settings like Fallout , Marvel , and Warhammer have featured types of power armor which tend to be technologically enhanced suits of armor.",
   "scene": 3,
   "section": "Aesthetic design"
  },
  {
